[04/10/26]	molecule.h molecule.c
		Fixed "cartoon"/"ribbons" being impractically slow (minutes,
		sometimes never finishing) on very large multi-chain
		structures (8J07: 1546 chains, 3.1M atoms), via two separate
		O(n^2)-ish algorithms neither of which had ever been exercised
		at this scale before:
		 - CalcProteinHBonds() searched for a hydrogen-bonded partner
		   by scanning every other residue in the chain, checking a
		   CA-CA distance cutoff (MaxHDist, 9A) on each one. Replaced
		   with a spatial hash grid (cell size == the cutoff, so any
		   pair in range is guaranteed to land in the same or an
		   immediately neighbouring cell - the standard cell-list
		   technique): built fresh per chain from its own amino-acid
		   alpha-carbons, queried instead of scanned. Turns an
		   O(chain-length^2) search into O(chain-length). Caught and
		   fixed a bug of my own along the way: two distinct
		   neighbour cells can hash to the same bucket in a fixed-size
		   table, and visiting a colliding bucket twice silently
		   double-counted (and double-bonded) whatever lived in it;
		   fixed by deduplicating the up-to-27 neighbour bucket
		   indices before searching. Verified exact H-bond/Helix/
		   Strand/Turn count parity against the un-optimised code on
		   1mbn, 7dns and 1bmf before and after.
		 - FindBetaSheets()'s TestLadder() helper searches for a beta-
		   strand pairing partner by walking every residue in every
		   chain *after* the current one in the database - completely
		   independent of H-bond data, so the grid fix above didn't
		   help it at all. Since a ladder partner must be hydrogen
		   bonded (hence within the same distance cutoff), precompute
		   each protein chain's alpha-carbon bounding box once per
		   FindBetaSheets() call and skip any later chain whose
		   cutoff-expanded bounding box can't possibly contain a
		   partner, without changing TestLadder's internal residue
		   walk at all (lower risk, given it's hand-tuned legacy code
		   with pre-existing maybe-uninitialized warnings elsewhere in
		   the same function). This was the dominant remaining cost:
		   full "cartoon" on 8J07 (every one of the 1546 chains) now
		   completes in 12.7s total (~7s of actual computation beyond
		   the 5.4s load), versus needing to be killed before as it
		   hadn't finished after several minutes. Verified exact
		   Helix/Strand/Turn parity on 1mbn, 7dns and 1bmf (1bmf has
		   real multi-chain structure, so this exercised the new
		   cross-chain skip, not just a no-op).

[04/10/26]	molecule.h
		Widened Atom.serno and Group.serno from short (16-bit, max
		32767) to Long (32-bit), after a user report of a visibly
		wrong negative atom number ("Atom: C -15273") on the same
		8J07 structure (3,146,111 atoms - PDB's own fixed-width
		columns already overflow a 16-bit field, mmCIF just has no
		ceiling at all to mask it). This was a latent bug in the
		original 1995 code, only now actually reachable because
		mmCIF removes the size limits PDB always had. Removed a
		(short) truncating cast in the mmCIF atom-serial assignment
		that would have silently reimposed the bug even after the
		struct field was widened. Verified with a synthetic mmCIF
		file using an atom id (3146111) and residue number (70000)
		both deliberately beyond 32767: "select atomno=3146111" and
		"select 70000" now each correctly select exactly one atom,
		and "show sequence" prints the real residue number instead of
		a wrapped negative one.

[04/10/26]	molecule.h molecule.c infile.c abstree.h abstree.c command.c
		render.c repres.c script.c transfor.c
		Widened Chain.ident from a single char to a string (char
		ident[MAXCHAINID], MAXCHAINID=8 in molecule.h), so the mmCIF
		reader no longer has to truncate auth_asym_id to its first
		character - previously chains "AA" and "AB" (common in large
		complexes: cryo-EM assemblies, AlphaFold-Multimer, viral
		capsids) silently collided into the same RasMol chain "A".
		Added Chain.identval (Long), the first 4 characters packed
		into one int, computed once in CreateChain(); this is what
		the selection language's generic property-comparison engine
		(EvaluateProperty's PropChain case) actually compares, since
		that machinery only knows how to compare scalar ints for
		every property (residue number, atom name, chain, ...) -
		selecting by chain name beyond 4 characters just doesn't
		disambiguate on the extra characters, which is not a
		realistic limit for any real structure. Updated every call
		site: the PDB and CHARMM loaders (which only ever produce
		single character idents - added a ChainIdentIs() helper in
		infile.c for their comparisons), the mmCIF loader (now uses
		the full auth_asym_id/label_asym_id string), both places that
		parse "chain" out of the selection language ("123:AB" in
		command.c's ParseRange, and the PDB zone-string mini-grammar
		"RES123:AB.ATOM" in abstree.c's ParsePrimitiveExpr - both now
		consume a run of alnum characters instead of exactly one),
		and every display/export path (FormatLabel's %c/%s, "show
		sequence", PickAtom/DescribeAtom, the cartoon label-template
		chooser in repres.c, MolScript/Kinemage export in script.c).
		Two places are structurally stuck at one character regardless
		and are left truncating to the first character, documented in
		place: SavePDBMolecule (real PDB format's chain column is
		physically one character) and the legacy, rarely used PDB
		COLOR record mask-matching extension in transfor.c.
		Caught and fixed a real bug surfaced while testing this:
		ChainIdentValue()'s packing loop read name[i] independently
		at each of the 4 positions instead of latching "ended" at the
		first NUL, so uninitialized stack/heap bytes *after* a short
		chain name's terminator could leak into the packed comparison
		value. This silently broke "select X and :A"-style queries
		(any selection combining a chain with anything else) for
		ordinary single-character chains too, whenever the stale
		memory differed between where the value was built and where
		it was compared - a real, independently-discovered bug, not
		specific to multi-character chains. Verified with a synthetic
		3-chain ("A"/"AA"/"AB") mmCIF file: correct chain/atom counts,
		no collisions, "select :AA", "select 1:AB", "select protein
		and :A" and the zone-string form all distinguish chains
		correctly, and the full PDB/mmCIF regression suite (1crn,
		1mbn, 7dns, the AlphaFold P05067 prediction) is unchanged.

[04/10/26]	sdlwin.c
		Fixed a SIGSEGV when live-resizing the SDL window by dragging
		an edge (reported as "always crashes on mouse-up when
		launched with -size, but not from the default launch size").
		TransferImage() blits FBuffer into Texture using the *current*
		XRange as the source pitch, but XRange/YRange are updated the
		instant SDL_WINDOWEVENT_RESIZED/SIZE_CHANGED arrives, while
		Texture and FBuffer are only reallocated to match later, in
		CreateImage() (called from RefreshScreen()'s ReSizeScreen()).
		macOS's live-resize drag fires SDL_WINDOWEVENT_EXPOSED
		repeatedly *during* the drag, and that handler called
		TransferImage() directly - outside the normal resize-then-
		redraw sequence - so it would blit at the new (larger) pitch
		against the still old-sized Texture/FBuffer, overreading the
		FBuffer heap allocation. This is not actually specific to
		-size (same race exists at the default size), but a bigger
		or non-default starting size makes it far more likely the
		overread lands on an unmapped page instead of adjacent heap
		slack, so it crashes reliably instead of silently corrupting
		nearby memory. Fixed by having TransferImage() check the
		Texture's actual dimensions (SDL_QueryTexture) against
		XRange/YRange and skip the blit if they don't match yet; the
		pending resize's own RefreshScreen() (which already runs
		within the same ~20ms HandleEvents() tick, since mouse-drag
		responsiveness depends on it) redraws correctly once buffers
		are resized, so no visible stall.

[03/10/26]	rasmol.c
		Added a "-size WIDTHxHEIGHT" (or "-size N" for a square
		window) command-line option, since the compiled-in default
		window size (576x576, from graphics.h's InitialWide/
		InitialHigh) is cramped on modern high-DPI displays and
		previously required a manual resize every launch. Window
		dimensions now flow through new WindowWide/WindowHigh
		variables (defaulting to InitialWide/InitialHigh, so nothing
		changes if the flag isn't given) instead of main() passing
		the InitialWide/InitialHigh macros straight to OpenDisplay().
		Rejects malformed or out-of-range (<40 or >8192 per side)
		sizes with the usage message, same as other bad flags.

[03/10/26]	rasmol.c
		Fixed main()'s interactive command loop spinning at 100% CPU
		forever once stdin reaches EOF without an "exit"/"quit" ever
		being issued (e.g. a piped/automated session, or closing the
		terminal with Ctrl-D) - ReadCharacter()'s EOF (-1) was never
		checked, so FetchCharacter()/getc(stdin) kept returning EOF
		millions of times a second with nothing to stop the loop.
		Compounding this, the loop variable "ch" was declared char,
		which is unsigned on this aarch64 target, so EOF (-1) got
		silently truncated to 255 before any check could even see it
		- widened it to int and added a "ch==EOF -> break" check,
		which reaches the same RasMolExit() a "quit" command does.
		This is independent of -script: LoadScriptFile() (command.c)
		reads script files through its own getc(fp) loop, so sourcing
		a script of predefined selections/styles and then continuing
		interactively (-script setup.rsc, no quit at the end of the
		script) is unaffected; only the real stdin stream's own EOF
		is now handled.

[03/10/26]	rasmol.c
		Fixed a double-free that aborted RasMol (SIGABRT, glibc
		"double free detected in tcache") at the end of almost any
		-script run. main()'s own fclose(fp) right after calling
		LoadScriptFile(fp,...) was closing a FILE* that
		LoadScriptFile() (command.c) already closes itself on every
		return path (end of script, "exit" command, or process exit
		via "quit"/RasMolExit()), so the handle's heap buffer got
		freed twice. Confirmed fixed, and the rest of the run is
		clean, under valgrind --error-exitcode. Spotted this while
		investigating what looked like mmCIF reader crashes, but the
		bug is unrelated to parsing and just as reproducible loading
		a plain PDB file via -script.

[03/10/26]	rasmol.c command.c infile.c transfor.h
		Cleaned up every warning the modern gcc/SDL2 build was
		producing (make now builds silent). Added the missing
		SetTraceTemperature() prototype to transfor.h (was an
		implicit-int declaration in command.c); added a top level
		<string.h> include to rasmol.c (strcmp etc. were implicitly
		declared outside the VMS build); fixed select()'s unused
		fd_set* arguments casting to a nonexistent "struct fd_set"
		type instead of the real fd_set typedef; added braces round
		an if/for/if-else chain in infile.c's ProcessFeatures() that
		gcc flagged as a dangling-else trap; gave SavePDBMolecule's
		"ch" TER-record variable an initial value to resolve a
		maybe-uninitialized warning (prev/ch are always set together,
		so this was a flow-analysis false positive, not a real bug);
		replaced the strncpy() calls added for the mmCIF loop-row
		reader with a small memcpy based helper, since GCC's
		stringop-truncation check can't see the explicit NUL write
		that already followed; and reworked InitHelpFile's priming
		fgets() so its result is actually inspected, since glibc's
		fortified fgets is marked warn_unused_result in a way a bare
		(void) cast does not silence.

[03/10/26]	infile.c infile.h command.c command.h rasmol.c
		Added a dependency-free mmCIF reader (LoadCIFMolecule, in
		infile.c), since RasMol previously only knew how to write
		CIF (SaveCIFMolecule) and not read it. Implements a small
		tag/loop tokeniser for the subset of the mmCIF grammar the
		PDB/AlphaFold actually emit ('#' comments, quoted strings,
		';' multi-line text fields) and structurally parses the
		"_atom_site" loop by column name rather than fixed column
		position, since mmCIF is tag/loop based, not column based
		like PDB. Atom/element typing is built to match the same
		4-column convention ComplexAtomType()/GetElemNumber() use
		for PDB files (justified by the explicit type_symbol rather
		than guessed from column position), so CIF-loaded structures
		get the same backbone tracing, cartoons and element colouring
		as PDB ones. A few summary fields (_entry.id, _struct.title,
		_cell.*, _symmetry.*) are also read to populate the same
		fields HEADER/COMPND/CRYST1 fill in for PDB. Hooked up via
		the existing (previously write-only) FormatCIF plumbing in
		command.c's FetchFile.
		Also added GuessMoleculeFormat() (command.c/command.h),
		since without it a bare "load foo.cif" or "rasmol foo.cif"
		silently defaulted to the fixed-column PDB parser, which
		misreads free-form CIF text as 80-column fields and produces
		garbage, visually flattened coordinates. Format is now
		guessed from the .cif/.mmcif extension whenever the user
		doesn't say otherwise, in both the "load" command and the
		command-line filename argument; explicit "-cif"/"load cif"
		are unaffected. Added "-cif" to the -help usage line.

[03/10/26]	sdlwin.c rasmol.h rasmol.c Makefile Makefile.in
		Replaced the X11/Xlib display backend (x11win.c) with a new
		SDL2 backend (sdlwin.c), so RasMol no longer needs an X
		server (ie. XQuartz on macOS) to run. x11win.c drew its own
		menu bar, scrollbars and dials-box by hand with raw Xlib
		calls on top of blitting the renderer's framebuffer; the
		menu/scrollbar/dials-box chrome has been dropped in favour
		of RasMol's existing command language, but mouse-driven
		rotate/translate/zoom/slab, click-to-pick and window resize
		all still work and were ported across unchanged in spirit.
		Also fixed Card/Long/Pixel (rasmol.h) to be pinned to 32 bits
		via _LONGLONG, since plain "long" is 64 bits on modern LP64
		systems and was silently corrupting the pixel-packing code;
		switched on THIRTYTWOBIT (24-bit true colour) as the default
		since 256-colour displays are now obsolete. Tightened the
		stdin/event poll in rasmol.c from 1s to 20ms while a display
		window is open, for responsive dragging. Makefile/Makefile.in
		now build against SDL2 (via sdl2-config) instead of hardcoded
		per-workstation X11 flags from 1995.

